  "intent": "Hold the best measured geometry from iteration 8 (sensor y 0.075, 12 mm footprint, wheel_track 0.09, body_length 0.10, mass 0.6, wheel_radius 0.03, body_width 0.12) and change nothing. The purpose is to give the Integrator a stable platform so that a controller change (kd 0.6 or kp 2.5, per the feedback) can be judged cleanly against the 668 baseline. Expected result: at least the iteration 8 score, with s-bend 19 or more finishes and oval 20 of 20. The slalom should improve only if the controller can hold the line through its alternating bends. If the slalom stays at 0 finishes at its max_error floor, geometry is at its limit for the development tracks.",
  "feedback_request": {
    "measurements": ["completion", "progress", "completion_time", "rms_error", "max_error", "line_loss_events", "steering_oscillation", "control_effort", "noise_robustness"],
    "questions": [
      "Did the s-bend keep 19 or more finishes with line losses at or below 0.8 (the iteration 8 value)? A drop below 0.55 would confirm the look-ahead and inertia gains are still available.",
      "Did the slalom finish any trials, or did its progress rise above 0.34 with line losses below 1.1? Slalom is the only track with no finishes.",
      "Did oval max_error stay near 0.0165 with 20 of 20 finishes, so that the controller change adds no lag or jitter?",
      "Does steering_oscillation stay under about 0.35 on the s-bend and slalom with the controller change? If it rises, the controller gain is too high and geometry is not the cause."
    ]
  },
  "learning": "Iterations 5 to 8 show that yaw inertia (body_length and mass) and look-ahead (y 0.07 to 0.075) are the geometry levers that moved the s-bend and slalom. Mass 0.5 reversed the gain, and y 0.075 raised slalom progress without finishes. The remaining limit appears to be turn authority on alternating bends, which geometry has largely reached. Controller gain is the next lever, and this design is held fixed for that test.",
